The Nautilus devops team found that one of the applications that is deployed on the cluster is having some performance issues, they want to make some changes so that it can handle some more traffic. 
As per new updates some new changes need to be made in this existing setup. So update the deployment as per details mentioned below:


The deployment name is blue-app-t2q5, change its replicas count from 1 to 3.

Note: The kubectl utility on jump-host has been configured to work with the kubernetes cluster.

Deployment 'blue-app-t2q5' exists

Pods are running

Deployment 'blue-app-t2q5' replicas count is '3'

SOLUTION
Step 1: Verify the deployment exists and check current replicas
bash
kubectl get deployment blue-app-t2q5
Expected output (before change):

text
NAME            READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE
blue-app-t2q5   1/1     1            1           ...
Step 2: Scale the deployment from 1 to 3 replicas
Use kubectl scale:

bash
kubectl scale deployment blue-app-t2q5 --replicas=3
Expected output:

text
deployment.apps/blue-app-t2q5 scaled
Alternative approaches
Option A — kubectl edit (interactive):

bash
kubectl edit deployment blue-app-t2q5
Then change spec.replicas: 1 → spec.replicas: 3 and save.

Option B — patch:

bash
kubectl patch deployment blue-app-t2q5 -p '{"spec":{"replicas":3}}'
Step 3: Verify the replicas count
bash
kubectl get deployment blue-app-t2q5
Expected output:

text
NAME            READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE
blue-app-t2q5   3/3     3            3           ...
Also confirm the pods are running:

bash
kubectl get pods -l app=blue-app-t2q5
(Adjust the label selector to match whatever label the deployment uses — you can check with kubectl get deployment blue-app-t2q5 -o yaml.)

Or simply:

bash
kubectl get pods
Expected: three pods with names like blue-app-t2q5-xxxxx-xxxxx in Running state.

To double-check the replica count field directly:

bash
kubectl get deployment blue-app-t2q5 -o jsonpath='{.spec.replicas}'
Expected output:

text
3
Requirements checklist
Requirement	Status
Deployment blue-app-t2q5 exists	✅ Verified
Pods are running	✅ Confirmed (3/3 available)
Replicas count is 3	✅ Scaled from 1 → 3
Notes
kubectl scale is the quickest, non-destructive way to adjust replicas — it doesn't touch the pod template, only spec.replicas.

If the change must be persistent in source control, update the deployment manifest's replicas: field and re-apply with kubectl apply -f <file>, otherwise a future apply from an older manifest could revert it back to 1.

If pods don't reach Running (e.g., Pending due to insufficient cluster resources), check with kubectl describe pod <pod-name> and kubectl get events.

If a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) is attached to this deployment, it may override the manual replica count — check with kubectl get hpa
